## Paper Assessment

**Paper:** SELECT — Satisficing Regret Minimization in Bandits

### Dimension-by-Dimension Comparison

**Novelty (strong):** The paper fills a genuine gap. Prior satisficing bandit work (Michel et al. 2023, Garivier et al. 2019) only works for finite-armed settings with a positive satisficing gap. SELECT's key insight is to use the "exceeding gap" Δ*_S instead, enabling constant satisficing regret for Lipschitz and concave bandits (where satisficing gap = 0). The algorithmic template — oracle trajectory sampling + forced sampling + LCB testing — is a clean and novel combination. Comparable in novelty to 0T49QbSOho (list replicability) and stronger than Lipschitz-in-optimal-space (i7k2sXSW1b), which was a novelty variant on a single metric.

**Technical Soundness (good):** The proof structure is sound. Propositions 1 and 2 together lead to the main theorem, and the geometric argument (shifted geometric r.v.) is elegant. The non-realizable case follows directly from the oracle guarantee. The lower bounds for finite-armed and concave bandits complete the picture. One minor gap: the upper bound for finite-armed bandits is O(K/Δ*) while the lower bound is Ω(1/Δ*), leaving a factor of K unaccounted. This is a moderate weakness but not fatal.

**Empirical Support (solid):** Experiments across three bandit types (finite-armed, concave, Lipschitz), both realizable and non-realizable cases, with comparison to SAT-UCB, SAT-UCB+, and oracles. Results convincingly demonstrate constant satisficing regret. Better empirical support than 0T49QbSOho (no experiments) and comparable to ptjrpEGrGg. Slightly narrower than Oeb0I3JcVc which used real-world datasets.

**Significance (good):** Achieving constant satisficing regret for Lipschitz and concave bandits is a meaningful advance. The general template means the result applies to any problem where a sublinear oracle exists — a broad class. The practical motivation (satisficing in decision-making) is well-articulated. Similar in significance to ptjrpEGrGg and slightly below Oeb0I3JcVc which had broader algorithmic implications.

**Clarity (very good):** Well-structured paper. The three algorithmic steps are clearly motivated. Key remarks explain why each design choice (LCB vs UCB, forced sampling) is essential and how it differs from prior work. Better written than C9YyVygCpG (confusing algorithm description) and i7k2sXSW1b (poor presentation).

### Relative Scoring

| Paper | Decision | Avg Score |
|---|---|---|
| Geometry-Aware Linear Bandits (Oeb0I3JcVc) | Accept Poster | ~7.0 |
| LIHF Corruption-Robust Dueling (ptjrpEGrGg) | Accept Poster | ~6.4 |
| List Replicable Bandits (0T49QbSOho) | Accept Poster | ~6.25 |
| Max-Min Fair Bandit (C9YyVygCpG) | Reject | ~5.75 |
| Private Adversarial Bandits (SbV2eJC7Ci) | Reject | ~5.67 |
| Lipschitz Optimal Space (i7k2sXSW1b) | Accept Poster | ~5.25 |

SELECT is clearly above the Reject boundary. It is broader in scope than list replicable bandits (0T49QbSOho), has cleaner algorithm design and motivation than LIHF (ptjrpEGrGg), and has tight, well-explained experiments. The upper-lower bound gap (O(K/Δ*) vs Ω(1/Δ*) for finite arms) is a notable weakness, and the non-realizable case result is essentially trivial (run the oracle). These prevent it from reaching the level of Oeb0I3JcVc (avg ~7.0). I place it between ptjrpEGrGg and Oeb0I3JcVc, closer to the lower end of that range, around **6.6**.

