## Scoring Rationale

**LinBridge** proposes a Jacobian-factorization framework that decomposes nonlinear visual encoding models into a "linear inherent component" and per-sample "nonlinear mapping biases," using contrastive learning (InfoNCE) to extract these components from fMRI data.

### Dimension-by-dimension assessment

| Dimension | Assessment |
|---|---|
| **Novelty** | High — Jacobian factorization via contrastive learning is a genuine conceptual contribution; AFD metric for cortical nonlinearity quantification is new |
| **Technical soundness** | Moderate — contrastive loss optimization dynamics unanalyzed, the mechanism by which JM_inherent generates R² predictions is unclear, no ablation vs. simpler baselines |
| **Empirical support** | Weak-to-moderate — single architecture (2-layer MLP), predominantly one subject; narrower than accepted papers |
| **Significance** | Moderate — hierarchical nonlinearity finding is neuroscientifically interesting, but proof-of-concept only |
| **Clarity** | Moderate — core ideas explained but key derivations and forward-pass mechanism underspecified |

### Calibration comparison

- **Accepted posters** (BrainSAIL ~6.4 avg, SIM ~6.5 avg, At9JmGF3xy ~5.75 avg) all demonstrate more robust multi-subject or multi-dataset validation and cleaner methodology.
- **Rejected papers with interesting concepts but narrow scope** (hgBVVAJ1ym ~5.3 avg, veyPSmKrX4 ~5.75 avg) are the closest comparators.
- LinBridge's novelty is stronger than most of these rejected papers, but the methodological gaps (unverified contrastive loss justification, single-architecture scope) are substantial — similar severity to what sank hgBVVAJ1ym and veyPSmKrX4.

The paper sits at the low end of the borderline range — genuine novelty and reasonable setup, but insufficient justification and empirical breadth to reach acceptance. A score of **4.5** reflects real contribution above clearly weak papers, while recognizing the multiple unresolved methodological concerns.

