## Comparative Analysis

### Paper Summary
Pure empirical comparison of KAN vs MLP on 10 synthetic 1D functions organized into six regularity categories (regular, non-differentiable, jump, singular, oscillatory, noisy), with timing data and a noise analysis.

---

### Dimension-by-Dimension Comparison

**Novelty**
The 6-category taxonomy gives some organizational originality, but the core question ("does KAN beat MLP on irregular functions?") was already being explored in prior work cited by the authors (Shen et al., Yu et al., Tran et al.). No new architecture or method is proposed. The noise-saturation insight (Section 4.3) is the one genuinely non-obvious finding. This is below PigfMZMHq1 (which at least introduces KAN into a previously unexplored domain) and similar to wYVP4g8Low (which at least proposes LCN, however incrementally).

**Technical Soundness**
This is the paper's most serious failure area. A critical text–figure contradiction in one of the four core findings (jump functions), a dangling "section D" reference indicating an incomplete manuscript, an undefined noise model (distribution, scale, additive/multiplicative, clean vs. noisy test targets all unspecified), and single-run experiments with no variance reporting collectively undermine the reliability of every numerical claim. Bb1ddVX8rL also had contradictory figures (scored ~3.5) and was flagged similarly; the current paper's level of internal inconsistency is at least as severe.

**Empirical Support**
Only 1D functions are tested, missing the multivariate regime where KAN's spline structure actually matters theoretically. Two architectures × ten functions × two training sizes, all as single runs — this is the thinnest acceptable level for a workshop paper, let alone ICLR. wYVP4g8Low (avg 3.0) was rejected partly for "mixed and not consistently better" results with shallow analysis; the current paper's empirical scope is comparable.

**Significance**
The wall-clock timing data (Tables 3–4) showing KAN is 10–70× slower is a practically significant finding that many KAN papers omit. The noise-saturation finding is potentially interesting. However, the taxonomy (while useful) covers known categories of irregular functions, and the conclusion is framed misleadingly (claiming KAN's "faster convergence" in epochs without acknowledging the wall-clock penalty). Significance is below KAAN/3VOKrLao5g (which provides a new theoretical perspective on MLP–KAN duality), but modestly above wYVP4g8Low.

**Clarity**
The organization is reasonably clear at a high level, but critical details are absent (noise model, experimental protocol). The Section D reference indicates the paper was submitted incomplete. The text–figure contradiction in Section 3.3 / Figure 3 caption is a writing/proofreading failure that no reviewer can overlook.

---

### Calibration Placement

| Calibration paper | Avg score | Key similarity |
|---|---|---|
| **wYVP4g8Low** (LCNs) | ~3.0 | Empirical KAN comparison, methodology gaps, no ablations |
| **Bb1ddVX8rL** (Legendre-KAN) | ~3.5 | Internal figure contradictions, writing issues |
| **PigfMZMHq1** (PointNet-KAN) | ~3.67 | Naive empirical KAN comparison, limited novelty |
| **3VOKrLao5g** (KAAN) | ~4.25 | Stronger theory + empirical; clearly higher bar |
| **ydlDRUuGm9** (KAN expressiveness) | ~6.25 | Accepted; substantially more rigorous and novel |

The current paper sits between wYVP4g8Low and PigfMZMHq1. The taxonomy and timing contributions are slightly stronger than LCN, but the internal contradiction and incompleteness (missing section D) are more damaging than anything in PointNet-KAN. Compared to Bb1ddVX8rL (which at least proposes a new variant), the current paper provides only a comparison without a novel method, which is a weaker contribution for the venue. The multiple combined major flaws (contradiction, undefined noise model, single runs, missing section) push it toward the lower end of this range.

**Score: ~3.2**

